\name{sdrf_read}
\alias{sdrf_read}
\title{Read one SDRF-Proteomics experimental-design file}
\usage{
sdrf_read(path, limit = NULL, offset = 0, timeout = 60)
}
\arguments{
\item{path}{Path to a \code{.sdrf.tsv} file.}

\item{limit}{Maximum rows to return. \code{NULL}, the default, returns all of them.}

\item{offset}{Rows to skip.}

\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow, or \code{NULL} to wait indefinitely.
}

}
\description{
Read one SDRF-Proteomics file as its header and rows of raw cells, keeping repeated column names and ragged rows exactly as written.

Every other reader here answers what a search \strong{found}. SDRF answers what was \strong{searched} -
which sample, which organism part, which replicate, which instrument settings - which is the
half you need to compare results across experiments.
}
\details{
\strong{Use this, not \code{\link{readers_read_records}}, for SDRF.} \code{readers_read_records()} joins each row's
cells into one semicolon-separated string, and SDRF's own \code{NT=...;AC=...} grammar puts
semicolons inside cells, so that string cannot be split back apart.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_sdrf}: \code{path}, \code{columns} (a character vector that may repeat), \code{rows} (a
  list of character vectors, one per row), \code{row_count} (the whole document), \code{returned_count},
  \code{offset}, \code{truncated} and \code{caveats}.

}
\section{The shape is row-major, by necessity}{


SDRF column names are data, and they \strong{repeat}: 649 files in the curated corpus carry
\code{comment[modification parameters]} more than once, up to eight times in one file. A data.frame
would force those names unique. So \code{columns} keeps the duplicates, \code{rows} holds the cells, and
position links them. Use \code{\link{sdrf_value}} for the first cell under a name and \code{\link{sdrf_all}} for
every one.

\strong{Rows are ragged.} A row may carry fewer cells than \code{columns} has entries - PXD059974 in
mzLib's own fixtures has a 46-column header with 17 of its 22 rows carrying 42 cells - and mzLib
preserves that so the file round-trips. \code{\link{sdrf_value}} gives \code{NA} for a position a row does not
reach.

\strong{Cells are raw strings, never interpreted.} The key=value grammar arrives exactly as written,
because it cannot be told apart from a \code{comment[file uri]} cell whose pre-signed URL contains
\code{Signature=} and \code{Expires=}.

\strong{A reserved word is a real value.} \code{"not available"} and \code{"not applicable"} mean the
experiment stated an absence. \code{NA} means the document has no such column. Do not collapse them.

}
\section{It does not validate yet}{


mzLib models SDRF's structural rules in \code{SdrfValidator} and its vocabulary-drift rules in
\code{SdrfDriftLint}. Both have been public since mzLib #1207, which the pinned bridge includes, but
the bridge does not expose them yet. When it does, they will be projected here from mzLib rather
than reimplemented. Until then this makes no claim about whether a document is correct.

}
